#{[17592186075029 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a number, its **additive inverse** is the number we need to add to get zero.\nTo find the additive inverse of a number, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in front of it. \n\nFor example, to find the additive inverse of $5,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-}}\\,(5) = -5.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n5+(-5) = 5-5 = 0 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075029 #edb/ref 1065 15159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075029 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "21fd73d9-f9b8-5ef8-9e18-9ba21d6d8d79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075029 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075030 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the additive inverse of a negative number, we can use the same process.\n\nFor instance, to find the additive inverse of the number $-3,$ we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in front of it.\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-}}\\,(-3) = 3.\n$$\n\nAgain, we check our work by adding the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n(-3)+3 = -3+3 = 0 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075030 #edb/ref 1065 15161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075030 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d30313d5-d29e-5eea-b11c-0619439becb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075030 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Integer" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075038 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the following equivalent ratios:\n\n![](images/6c/6cda61e0fbd0d9695644a29f0c6fa3478eae84cf253623da7f4b544f35873472.svg)\n\nWe can place the equivalent ratios in a **ratio table**, such as the one below. In a ratio table, each row represents a ratio.\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\textrm{?}}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nOne number is missing. Let's figure out what it is.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10},$ which is the first ratio in the table. Writing this ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}10}}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}10}$).\n\nTo find the missing number, we need to make a numerator of ${\\color{blue}10}.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\times 2}{10\\times 2} & =\\frac{10}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}20}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}20.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075038 #edb/ref 1065 8357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075038 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca7b3d51-7281-5705-bed5-276f34939c25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075038 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109303 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109303 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109303 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186109308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109303 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e044e866-6811-5b05-95ac-6974da60d777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109304 #edb/ref 1080 "$Q$ and $S$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17