ef 17592186120941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120026 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120026 #edb/ref 1045 "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120026 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120026 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120026 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120026 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "18514bce-156b-56a7-b919-0aa8cd6b4e23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120934 #edb/ref 1061 554687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc943f83-ca68-59a5-8d04-1f3be7f4da3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120935 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120935 #edb/ref 1061 554688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41a11614-97fd-50d6-ae34-9ed01ba0df21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120936 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120936 #edb/ref 1061 554692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120936 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1893b834-1078-578f-b788-398a16705469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120936 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120937 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120937 #edb/ref 1061 554696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b332403-62f5-5fe0-ac76-52a66e2c10b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120938 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120938 #edb/ref 1061 554697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120938 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b172821f-393c-54f4-bec3-98560d9d0245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120938 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120939 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120939 #edb/ref 1061 554701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120939 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b06bda82-f4c5-5f02-bffa-cfb125aada53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120939 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120940 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120940 #edb/ref 1061 554702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120940 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2768c295-f0d4-5823-a374-5050e8220981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120940 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120941 #edb/ref 1061 554705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120941 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9786bea-a98c-554e-ac43-50ebe1956098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120941 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121142 #edb/ref 1064 "Finding the **cube root** of a number is the reverse of cubing a number. We express the cube root using the **radical symbol** $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}.$\n\nFor example, the \"cube root of $8$\" equals $\\color{blue}2$ since \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^3 = {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, we write $\\sqrt[3]{8}={\\color{blue}2}.$\n\nSome basic cube roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{0} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{0}^{3}} & = & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{1}^{3}} & = & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{2}^{3}} & = & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{27} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{3}^{3}} & = & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{64} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{4}^{3}} & = & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{125} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}} & = & 5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{10}^{3}} & = & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA **perfect cube** is a number whose cube root is a whole number. So, the numbers \n\n$$\n0,\\qquad 1,\\qquad  8,\\qquad  27,\\qquad  64,\\qquad 125, \\qquad 1\\,000\n$$\n\n are all perfect cubes. You need to remember them." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121142 #edb/ref 1065 8377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121142 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "82bf149b-fb3b-506e-9c7b-3d6716081eaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121142 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121143 #edb/ref 1064 "Unlike the square root, it *is* possible to find the cube root of a negative number.\n\nFor example, $\\sqrt[3]{-8}={\\color{blue}{-2}}$ because\n\n$$\n-8 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})^3 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}}).\n$$\n\nSome basic cube roots of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-1} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{3}} & = & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-8} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-2)^{3}} & = & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-27} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}} & = & -3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-64} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-4)^{3}} & = & -4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-125} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-5)^{3}} & = & -5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-1\\,000} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-10)^{3}} & = & -10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the numbers \n\n$$\n-1,\\qquad  -8,\\qquad  -27,\\qquad  -64,\\qquad -125, \\qquad -1\\,000\n$$\n\nare all perfect cubes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121143 #edb/ref 1065 8378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121143 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "02c87b9c-9741-553f-a98b-da7832928f6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121143 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cube Root of a Negative Perfect Cube" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121144 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's calculate $(\\sqrt[3]{64})^3$ by performing each operation one step at a time:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\color{blue}64}\\right)^3 = \\left(\\sqrt[3]{{\\color{red}4}^3}\\right)^3 = {\\color{red}4}^3 = {\\color{blue}64}\n$$\n\nNotice that cubing the cube root gives the number under the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\sqrt[3]{64} \\right)^3 = 64\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. If $\\color{blue}\\square$ is any number, then\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\color{blue}\\square}\\right)^3 = {\\color{blue}\\square}.\n$$\n\nThe cube and the cube root cancel each other out, and we're left with the number that we started with.\n\n![](images/78/784f43f7253e1ed6fa394462a172e22571af06a5318b424ba2eab2157333c6fe.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121144 #edb/ref 1065 8379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121144 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "900df988-f097-53fe-8a55-39641f798d4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121144 #edb/ref 1067 "Cubing a Cube Root" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121145 #edb/ref 1064 "We can think of the cube root geometrically. For instance, let's represent the number $8$ by a collection of eight items:\n\n![](images/36/3677ea5b5f875e12fdcc6376f6b29b43a663495d20a3fbf69b4937cc61661d79.png)\n\nNow, we can pile them up in the form of a cube where each side is equal to $\\sqrt[3]{8}={\\color{blue}2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121145 #edb/ref 1065 8380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121145 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "859f5c79-ff9d-5b6d-91b3-305863a271c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121145 #edb/ref 1067 "A Geometric Approach to the Cube Root" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138536 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138536 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138536 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138536 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138536 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138536 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing \"Or\" Inequalities on Number Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138536 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138536 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6090342d-3d4e-519f-ab7d-3fb6df6e92a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying \"Or\" Inequalities Given on Number Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138537 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "32daa2fb-e5fa-5e44-b6f2-5d815d9145af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138538 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138538 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138538 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138538 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138538 #edb/ref 1039 "Representing \"And\" Inequalities on Number Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138538 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138538 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9106723c-b8b0-586e-8c2b-9bb4f50bc945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138539 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138539 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138539 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138539 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138539 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138539 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying \"And\" Inequalities Given on Number Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138539 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138539 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a707a741-0208-5a74-8478-ac05ff2673ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1045 "Compound Inequalities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186139522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138614 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4e2e5333-ed8e-576e-abed-94cc93efbdb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139522 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139522 #edb/ref 1061 79780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "73ffc612-4a54-5b2a-9949-3131ab4b5010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139523 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139523 #edb/ref 1061 79781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d905dee-c60b-587c-8b1e-96a234f4883a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139524 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139524 #edb/ref 1061 79784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139524 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bcf79e23-3398-56f2-9f7c-6dddc11d8b32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139524 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139525 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139525 #edb/ref 1061 79788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139525 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59dbf0dd-b3a9-5867-8cbf-3b28624f9ad9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139525 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139526 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139526 #edb/ref 1061 79789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139526 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e38370d-1c18-5d27-bb22-cf9444dd1b90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139526 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139527 #edb/ref 1061 79793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139527 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40b2748c-2bc7-5064-b788-ba5145c312f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139527 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139662 #edb/ref 1064 "A **compound inequality** is an inequality that consists of multiple different inequalities put together. For example, the compound inequality \n\n$$\nx \\leq 0 \\hspace{.25cm} \\text{ or } \\hspace{.25cm} x \\gt  3\n$$\n\n states that $x$ is less than or equal to $0$, or greater than $3$.\n\nFor the compound \"or\" inequality to be satisfied, at least one of the separate inequalities must be satisfied. To illustrate:\n\n- $x=-1$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies $x \\leq 0.$\n\n- $x=5$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies $x > 3.$\n\n- $x=1$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $x \\leq 0$ and it also does not satisfy $x > 3.$\n\nTo graph a compound inequality involving the word \"or,\" we graph the two separate inequalities on the same number line. In this particular case,\n\n- the inequality $x \\leq 0$ consists of a closed circle at $0$ and an arrow to the left, while\n\n- the inequality $x >3$ consists of an open circle at $3$ and an arrow to the right.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/96/96f1b1ee25ebc210a83ba12d90396683b7e2660050f820c4057ab85de419db91.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139662 #edb/ref 1065 8132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139662 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "770f70a7-6710-533c-bd9a-e37123e913bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139662 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139663 #edb/ref 1064 "Compound inequalities can also be written with the word \"and.\" However, such inequalities are often written together as a single expression with two inequality signs. For example, the compound inequality \n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x < -1\n$$\n\n means that $-4 \\leq x$ and $x < -1.$\n\nFor the compound \"and\" inequality to be satisfied, both of the separate inequalities must be satisfied. To illustrate:\n\n- $x=-3$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies both $-4 \\leq x$ and $x < -1.$\n\n- $x=-5$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $-4 \\leq x.$\n\n- $x=0$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $x < -1.$\n\nTo graph this compound inequality, we first graph the two inequalities separately.\n\n![](images/42/42edf8ee61badbbd1e5525a3843cfbe189e6432b2ad06455c1bdca6a7087ac56.png)\n\nThen, we keep only where they overlap.\n\n![](images/fa/fa07233f96bed30ddfcc5905a55a835763359cd34188e4d1c249c13b1d27d25c.png)\n\n**Note:** Any compound \"and\" inequality will always represent a segment between two points. So, it is not always necessary to draw both inequalities and find the overlap. To speed up the process, we can just draw the endpoints with open or closed circles as appropriate, and highlight the area between the endpoints." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139663 #edb/ref 1065 8133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139663 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9859e4f2-2267-558c-923f-35c348957ad9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139663 #edb/ref 1067 "Compound \"And\" Inequalities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149800 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149800 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [